\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {4}{\ignorespaces Ablating segment~A at inference on all 437 rows, weights and threshold ($\tau = 0.04$) unchanged across every row. The base rate is $0.563$. Deleting the taxa takes ranking from $0.912$ to $0.677$, and the empty-query arm---which is exactly the deployed filter's formulation, passage in and verdict out---reaches $0.641$, a tenth of the way from chance to the conditioned model. Per-seed standard deviations on AUPRC are $\le 0.027$.\relax }}{6}{table.caption.16}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:ablation}{{4}{6}{Ablating segment~A at inference on all 437 rows, weights and threshold ($\tau = 0.04$) unchanged across every row. The base rate is $0.563$. Deleting the taxa takes ranking from $0.912$ to $0.677$, and the empty-query arm---which is exactly the deployed filter's formulation, passage in and verdict out---reaches $0.641$, a tenth of the way from chance to the conditioned model. Per-seed standard deviations on AUPRC are $\le 0.027$.\relax }{table.caption.16}{}}
\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {5}{\ignorespaces Per-seed figures for \textsc  {Verifier-PC}, and the ensemble formed by averaging the three checkpoints' probabilities. Elsewhere in this paper the ensemble row is what is reported, and we name it as an ensemble rather than as a mean over seeds, because the two are different objects and an earlier version of this work conflated them.\relax }}{6}{table.caption.18}\protected@file@percent }
